frontend: fall back to the plain charset when iconv has no //TRANSLIT.

Every ID3v2 text tag came out EMPTY on musl: `--tt Titulo` wrote a TIT2 frame
holding the UTF-16 BOM and nothing else. With ID3TAGS_EXTENDED on, the default
text encoding is TENC_UTF16, so --tt/--ta/--tl/--ty/--tg/--tv were all affected.

toUtf16() writes the BOM into the buffer BEFORE checking whether
iconv_open("UTF-16LE//TRANSLIT", ...) succeeded, and on failure hands that
two-byte buffer back as the converted string. musl's iconv has no //TRANSLIT,
so the open always failed and the tag was always just the BOM. glibc and GNU
libiconv do have it, which is why glibc builds and macOS look fine.

Fall back to the plain charset name when the //TRANSLIT form is rejected.
Where //TRANSLIT works nothing changes; on musl the conversion now runs, and
the bytes match a glibc lame exactly.

--- a/frontend/parse.c
+++ b/frontend/parse.c
@@ -233,6 +233,7 @@
         if (dst != 0) {
             char* cur_code = nl_langinfo(CODESET);
             iconv_t xiconv = iconv_open("ISO_8859-1//TRANSLIT", cur_code);
+            if (xiconv == (iconv_t)-1) xiconv = iconv_open("ISO-8859-1", cur_code);
             if (xiconv != (iconv_t)-1) {
                 char* i_ptr = (char*)src;
                 char* o_ptr = dst;
@@ -259,6 +260,7 @@
         if (dst != 0) {
             char* cur_code = nl_langinfo(CODESET);
             iconv_t xiconv = iconv_open("UTF-16LE//TRANSLIT", cur_code);
+            if (xiconv == (iconv_t)-1) xiconv = iconv_open("UTF-16LE", cur_code);
             dst[0] = 0xff;
             dst[1] = 0xfe;
             if (xiconv != (iconv_t)-1) {
